[=#protocoloptions]
= Client Protocol Options =

Whether written using the native SQL Relay API, or a connector of some sort, SQL Relay apps generally communicate with SQL Relay using //SQLRClient//, the native SQL Relay client-server protocol.

 [[Image(../../images/sqlrelaynativeprotocol.png,nolink)]]

However, SQL Relay also supports the !MySQL, !PostgreSQL, Firebird, TDS (Microsoft SQL Server and SAP/Sybase), and Oracle client-server protocols.  When SQL Relay is configured to use one of these, it allows native applications to communicate directly with SQL Relay without modification, and without having to install any software on the client.

 [[Image(../../images/sqlrelaymysqlprotocol.png,nolink)]]

 [[Image(../../images/sqlrelaypostgresqlprotocol.png,nolink)]]

 [[Image(../../images/sqlrelayfirebirdprotocol.png,nolink)]]

 [[Image(../../images/sqlrelaytdsprotocol.png,nolink)]]

 [[Image(../../images/sqlrelayoracleprotocol.png,nolink)]]

In these configurations, SQL Relay becomes a transparent proxy.  Applications aimed at SQL Relay think that they're talking to their native database, but in fact, are talking to SQL Relay.

* [sqlrclientprotocol.html SQLRClient Protocol]
* [mysqlprotocol.html MySQL Protocol]
* [postgresqlprotocol.html PostgreSQL Protocol]
* [firebirdprotocol.html Firebird Protocol]
* [tdsprotocol.html TDS Protocol]
* [oracleprotocol.html Oracle Protocol]
* [multipleprotocols.html Multiple Protocols]

